# Mission Control — environment
#
# EVERY LINE HERE IS OPTIONAL. A checkout with no `.env` at all runs the whole
# product on the committed fixture, with a real agent, and `npm run verify`
# passes without any of it. That is the point: if it needs a token, it is not
# the demo.
#
# Loaded by `apps/gateway/src/env.ts`, whose import must stay FIRST in main.ts —
# in ESM every imported module's body runs before the importer's, so a
# `loadEnvFile` called any later is too late for every module-level
# `process.env` read in the gateway. An import sorter that moves that line down
# restores the bug with no error and no obviously wrong output.
#
# `tsx watch` does not reload this file. Restart the gateway after editing it.


# ── Going live ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# THIS IS THE SWITCH. Jira, Slack, Zoom and Confluence are read from a
# connection graph on disk, not from vendor APIs — a collector writes
# `graph.json` and `records/`, and pointing at its output is the whole of going
# live for those four surfaces. See docs/GRAPH-SCHEMA.md for the contract.
#
# Unset means `./fixtures`, which is committed and regenerated by
# `npm run fixture`.
#
# `/api/health` reports which directory it read, how many nodes and edges were
# in it, and which collector wrote it — check there first, because a collector
# that produced an empty file is otherwise indistinguishable from a quiet week.
# MC_GRAPH_DIR=/path/to/collector/output

# The workflow's own status names, mapped to our five. Optional — unset means
# the built-in defaults, which know "Code Review", "QA", "Blocked" and about a
# dozen others.
#
# WORTH DOING FIRST on a real export. `statusCategory` has three values, so any
# word that falls through to it makes `in_review` and `blocked` unreachable —
# a "Peer Review" ticket lands in `in_progress` and the lane looks slightly
# wrong with nothing failing. To see which words your export uses and which are
# falling through:
#
#   node scripts/inspect.mjs statuses
#
# It prints a starting JSON for the unmapped ones. Correct the targets, save it,
# and point this at it. Deliberately NOT inside MC_GRAPH_DIR: that directory is
# rebuilt in full on every collector run, so a mapping kept there is one the
# next refresh deletes.
# MC_STATUS_MAP=/path/to/status-map.json

# Where the vault lives — notes, the durable event log, the caches. Working
# state, never committed. Unset means `./vault`.
# MC_VAULT_DIR=/path/to/vault


# ── Miro ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# The one surface NOT read from MC_GRAPH_DIR, which is why it has a real
# connector. With a token set, `listConnectors` returns whatever is drawn on the
# live canvas — a different account of what depends on what from the reconciled
# graph — so dependency truth is taken from the graph and this serves Sources,
# the sticky half of a ceremony, and the records a citation opens.
#
# Read by the GATEWAY only; nothing in the browser reads a board id.
# `listConnectors`, `exportSnapshot` and the 30s canvas poll all use these.
#
# Note that dependency truth comes from the graph even when this is set. The
# board is evidence; `listConnectors` is not the reconciled result.
# MIRO_ACCESS_TOKEN=
# MIRO_BOARD_ID=

# Which Confluence space the skills gather from. Unset means `MC`.
# CONFLUENCE_SPACE_KEY=MC

# Which Slack channel the outbound sync posts to when nothing names one — the
# "MC-102 is now BLOCKED" fan-out and an accepted post_message. Unset means the
# fixture's `C-mc`, which exists only in mock mode.
# SLACK_DEFAULT_CHANNEL=


# ── The agent ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# The ladder, in order: the Claude CLI (your own login — no key, no credit, and
# what a fresh checkout gets), then ANTHROPIC_API_KEY, then Copilot in live
# mode, then a scripted stub. `node scripts/inspect.mjs health` says which one
# is about to answer.
#
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
# ANTHROPIC_MODEL=
# ANTHROPIC_EFFORT=
# ANTHROPIC_MAX_TOKENS=
#
# An alternative to ANTHROPIC_API_KEY, for a gateway that issues bearer tokens.
# ANTHROPIC_AUTH_TOKEN=
#
# Per-job model overrides. Each falls back to ANTHROPIC_MODEL. They exist
# because these three run unattended and a cheaper model is often the right
# trade for them, which is not true of the chat turn.
# ANTHROPIC_EXTRACT_MODEL=
# ANTHROPIC_INFER_MODEL=
# ANTHROPIC_SUMMARY_MODEL=
#
# OPENROUTER — the provider for a host nobody is logged into.
#
# The other three each assume something a container does not have: the Claude
# CLI authenticates from a developer's own login, Copilot from a gh session, and
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY is metered per person. A shared demo URL wants one key in one
# variable, answering for everybody who opens the link.
#
# Selected by MC_MODE=openrouter, and chosen INSTEAD of the ladder rather than
# inside it — a laptop with a Claude login must not silently spend somebody's
# OpenRouter credits, and a demo host must not quietly answer from a credential
# that is not there. No key at that mode falls to the scripted stub and says so.
#
# It runs WITHOUT the tool loop: the model is handed the rendered context and
# reasons over it. Cheap models are erratic at tool-calling and a stalled loop
# is a demo that hangs. The cost is follow-up retrieval, which is why this is a
# mode and not the default. See apps/gateway/src/openrouter.ts.
# OPENROUTER_API_KEY=
#
# Unset means `openrouter/free` — the free ROUTING POOL, not a pinned `:free`
# model, which can leave the pool without notice. Unlike `openrouter/auto` it
# never falls through to a paid model, so a shared key cannot quietly spend.
#
# PIN A MODEL FOR ANYTHING PEOPLE WILL WATCH. The pool's property — any free
# endpoint that is up — is also its hazard, and both failures were seen live:
# a tool-tuned model streamed `<dots_function_call> <invoke name="` into the
# answer, and a reasoning model returned a clean, empty, error-free stream
# because `reasoning: exclude` removed the only channel it wrote to.
#
# `supported_parameters` on https://openrouter.ai/api/v1/models rules both out:
# no `tools` cannot emit the first, no `reasoning` cannot produce the second.
# OPENROUTER_MODEL=mistralai/mistral-small-24b-instruct-2501
#
# Points the provider somewhere else. `node scripts/stub-openrouter.mjs` answers
# 429 twice and then streams, which is the only way to exercise the bounded
# backoff without either a credential or a real rate limit.
# OPENROUTER_BASE_URL=

# The Claude CLI path — the default provider, which needs no key at all.
# MAX_TURNS bounds a single structured request; it is a runaway guard, not a
# quality knob.
# CLAUDE_CLI_MODEL=
# CLAUDE_CLI_MAX_TURNS=
#
# Doubles as the proxy knob, and is what makes scripts/verify-providers.mts
# possible without a credential.
# ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL=
#
# Copilot. A token is optional — `useLoggedInUser` defaults to true — but if you
# set one it must be a real one: an accepted-and-ignored token produces the
# failure you cannot debug from outside, where start() and createSession() both
# succeed and only the first turn dies on `errorType: "authentication"`.
# GITHUB_TOKEN=
# COPILOT_MODEL=
#
# How structured output is requested. `auto` walks sdk-mcp → messages-api →
# copilot → prompt-json; naming one pins it and fails rather than falling
# through, because a pin that silently degrades is not a pin. Run
# `npx tsx scripts/probe-mcp.mts` to see which this machine allows — a
# workspace that forbids MCP is a real deployment, and nothing here may sit
# behind a capability policy can switch off.
# MC_STRUCTURED=auto


# ── Behaviour ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# READ-ONLY. On unless you explicitly turn it off, and the default is the point:
# every source is readable and none is writable, so an accepted proposal, an
# outbound Jira comment, a Miro snapshot and the Slack digest all refuse.
#
# It guards the `Connectors` object itself rather than each call site, so a
# write added later is covered without anybody remembering to cover it — the
# same argument that puts HUMAN_ONLY on the shared tool seam instead of in a
# prompt. The vault, the event log and proposals are unaffected: those are ours,
# nothing outside can see them, and a proposal writes nothing outward until it
# is accepted. So you still see exactly what *would* have happened.
#
# Only an unambiguous off/false/0/no disables it. A typo stays safe.
# MC_SAFE_MODE=on

# Meeting rooms, comma separated. Zoom attributes speech from a shared room to
# the ROOM, so a summary says "CATOR to post the requirements" and the extractor
# faithfully reports an owner nobody is. The flagship alert gates on a *named*
# owner exactly so it never fires on "someone should look at that", and a room
# is that wearing a name — so a room is stripped and a promise owned only by one
# fails the gate. `Jerry (Cator)` becomes `Jerry`; `CATOR / Sohrab / Jody`
# becomes `Sohrab / Jody`. Per-office, so there is no useful default.
# MC_MEETING_ROOMS=CATOR,Boardroom

# `mock` (default), `live`, or `openrouter`. Per-surface credentials decide what
# is real now, so this only picks the chat provider. It is NOT the switch —
# MC_GRAPH_DIR is.
# MC_MODE=mock

# THE WALKTHROUGH, for a URL somebody opens cold — a hosted demo, a review, a
# screen at a conference. It wraps the app in a welcome card, a one-page pitch,
# a simulated morning notification naming the real top three alerts, and a
# sticky strip of tips. None of those are the product, which is why this is the
# mirror image of MC_SAFE_MODE above: that is ON unless explicitly turned off,
# because what it prevents is irreversible; this is OFF unless explicitly turned
# on, because a typo has to fall to the app rather than to a demo.
#
# Only on/true/1/yes turn it on. Read by the GATEWAY and reported by
# /api/health, so the shell asks rather than being rebuilt — flipping this is a
# restart, not a redeploy.
#
# With it unset the shell renders AlertApp and nothing else: no route is added,
# nothing under alerts/ changes, and its stylesheet is outside the seventeen
# that scripts/verify-design.mts checks against the preview.
# MC_DEMO=off

# How long a walkthrough runs before it resets for whoever opens the link next.
# A RESET, NOT A LIMIT: a shared demo URL otherwise hands the second arrival the
# first one's wandering as their starting state. Enforced in the browser and not
# a security boundary — MC_SAFE_MODE is what stops anything being written.
#
# Unset means 20. Clamped to 1–240 rather than validated, because a mistyped 0
# would expire the session on the tick after it started, which reads as the app
# being broken rather than as the variable being wrong.
# MC_DEMO_MINUTES=20

# The scheduler: re-derive at 07:00 and 19:00, /standup at 08:00, /tidy at
# 22:00. It only ever reads and proposes — it posts nothing and writes no field.
# MC_SCHEDULER=on

# How often the canvas poll diffs the board's arrows. Miro webhooks do not fire
# on connectors, so this is the only way an arrow closing a loop is noticed.
# MC_CANVAS_POLL_MS=30000

# Shared secret for POST /api/webhooks/*. Unset means unauthenticated, which is
# fine on a laptop and not fine anywhere else.
# MC_WEBHOOK_SECRET=

# PORT=8787

# WHICH INTERFACE THE GATEWAY BINDS. Unset means 127.0.0.1, and that default is
# the hosting decision rather than an accident of one: docs/ROADMAP.md D4 settles
# this as single-tenant, on one machine inside the evidence boundary. It is why
# a notification carries a pointer and never a quote — the transcripts do not
# leave the machine holding them, and a gateway reachable from the network makes
# that a policy instead of a fact.
#
# SET THIS IF YOU RUN IN A CONTAINER, a devcontainer or WSL, where a loopback
# bind is unreachable from the host even with -p 8787:8787. Nothing else needs
# it: the shell, every documented curl and inspect.mjs all reach localhost.
#
# WHAT IT COSTS. There is NO AUTHENTICATION on this gateway — every route is
# open, the vault writes and POST /api/tools/:name included, and cors() allows
# every origin (docs/KNOWN-GAPS.md §3). A non-loopback value prints a warning at
# boot saying so. MC_WEBHOOK_SECRET guards /api/webhooks/* and nothing else.
#
# Do NOT set this to make a real vendor webhook reach you. The 07:00/19:00
# re-derive is the supported cadence for exactly that.
# MC_BIND=127.0.0.1

# Where the app lives, for the deep link a notification carries. A notification
# carries a POINTER and never a quote — the evidence does not leave the machine
# holding it — so this URL is the whole of what gets sent.
# MC_APP_URL=http://localhost:4200

# The Slack bot. Set it and the scheduled pass posts one message per run — the
# count, the worst NEW claim, and a button that opens the app. Unset, the review
# inbox carries the run alone, which is the default and always works.
#
# An INCOMING WEBHOOK, not a bot token, and that is what keeps it independent of
# where this is hosted: the button is a link, so Slack never posts anything back
# and there is no request URL to expose. Create one at
# https://api.slack.com/apps → your app → Incoming Webhooks.
#
# The message carries the claim and why it matters, never a citation.
# MC_SLACK_WEBHOOK_URL=https://hooks.slack.com/services/T…/B…/…

# Where the SHELL looks for the gateway. Read by the browser, so it must carry
# the VITE_ prefix — anything else is silently undefined at build time. Unset
# means http://localhost:8787.
# VITE_MC_GATEWAY=http://localhost:8787

# Only read by scripts/inspect.mjs and scripts/seed-miro.mjs, for pointing them
# at a gateway that is not on localhost.
# MC_GATEWAY=http://localhost:8787
# MC_SHELL=http://localhost:4200


# ── Read by a script, not by the gateway ─────────────────────────────────────
#
# scripts/fetch-jira-sprints.mts, and nothing else. programme_graph emits no
# sprint STATE — sprints are bare names on an issue — and the flagship finding
# fires when a commitment's container closes, so without this the alert the
# product is built on cannot fire on real data, silently. Jira's agile API has
# it. Flags override these if you would rather not put them in a file.
#
#   npx tsx scripts/fetch-jira-sprints.mts --board 42 --out sprints.json
#
# Run it with no --board to list the boards the account can see. Read-only: it
# calls GET /rest/agile/1.0 and writes a local file.
#
# JIRA_BASE_URL=https://your-org.atlassian.net
# JIRA_EMAIL=the-account-the-token-belongs-to@example.com
# JIRA_API_TOKEN=            # https://id.atlassian.com/manage-profile/security/api-tokens


# ── Not read by anything ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# SLACK_BOT_TOKEN, SLACK_SIGNING_SECRET, ZOOM_ACCOUNT_ID, ZOOM_CLIENT_ID,
# ZOOM_CLIENT_SECRET
#
# These were here when each surface was going to grow its own API client. They
# do not exist any more: those surfaces are read from MC_GRAPH_DIR, and a
# credential the code never reads is worse than a missing one — you fill it in,
# nothing changes, and you have no idea why.
#
# The Slack pair stays dead even though notifications now work: the transport is
# an incoming webhook (MC_SLACK_WEBHOOK_URL above), which needs neither a bot
# token nor a signing secret — nothing is ever posted back to us.
